@database "godot"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: godot.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Godot"
@{h1}Godot

This plugin lets agents build and test games in @{b}Godot 4@{ub} on this
machine, over two channels. The @{i}headless@{ui} channel starts the Godot
binary as a process: it creates projects, checks scripts for parse errors,
plays a scene for a number of frames, runs a GDScript against the project
and exports builds -- all with the editor closed. The @{i}editor@{ui}
channel talks to a Godot editor you have open, through the
@{tt}godot_mcp@{utt} addon the plugin installs into the project: the scene
tree, node properties, playing the game with a frozen clock, injected
input, screenshots and the error log.

It ships three agents -- @{tt}gamedev@{utt}, a coding harness for games
with a tester and Blender and image sub-agents, @{tt}godot_agent@{utt} for
direct work in an open project, and @{tt}gamedev_tester@{utt} -- plus three
skills. It has twelve tools, no hooks and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Getting a project ready  " link ready}
  @{"  The agents  " link agents}
  @{"  What to expect  " link expect}
  @{"  When something does not work  " link trouble}

For agents and developers
  Headless:  @{" status " link status}  @{" setup " link setup_tool}  @{" import_assets " link import_assets}  @{" check " link check}
             @{" run " link run}  @{" script " link script}  @{" export " link export}
  Editor:    @{" scene " link scene}  @{" node " link node}  @{" play " link play}  @{" observe " link observe}  @{" command " link command}
  @{"  Paths, processes and the socket  " link safety}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Known gaps  " link gaps}
@{body}
@endnode

@node ready "Getting a project ready"
@{h1}Getting a project ready

@{h2}1. Tell the plugin which Godot

Set @{tt}godot_binary@{utt} in the @{tt}godot@{utt} server entry (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}) to the full path of the executable. On
Windows that is the @{tt}..._console.exe@{utt}: the plain exe detaches and
hands back no output, so every headless tool would see silence. The plugin
does not search the PATH.

@{h2}2. Prepare the project, editor closed

Ask the agent, or call the tool yourself:

@{code}
godot_setup(project="my_game")
@{body}

This creates @{tt}data/workspace/my_game@{utt} with a minimal
@{tt}project.godot@{utt} if there is none, copies the addon into
@{tt}addons/godot_mcp@{utt}, enables it, and runs a headless import -- which
loads the addon, and the addon registers its runtime autoload
@{tt}MCPGameBridge@{utt}. Running it again is harmless. A project of your
own works the same way: put it below @{tt}data/workspace/@{utt} and run
setup on it.

@{h2}3. Open the project in Godot

Only for the editor tools. The addon starts listening on
@{tt}127.0.0.1:6550@{utt} when the editor loads it. @{tt}godot_status@{utt}
tells you whether the binary works and which project the editor has open.
@endnode

@node agents "The agents"
@{h1}The agents

All three are defined in the plugin's @{tt}agents/@{utt} folder and are
loaded with it; all are for users with the admin role only
(@{tt}min_role: admin@{utt}), because they can run code on this machine.

@{h2}gamedev

@{tt}type: coder@{utt}: it @{i}is@{ui} the coder harness -- same model
chain, same 300 steps, same file sandbox @{tt}coder_fs@{utt} and shell
@{tt}coder_shell@{utt} -- with a few swaps:

@{table}
What | Change
tools | adds @{tt}godot/*@{utt} and @{tt}media_ops/*@{utt}; @{tt}gamedev_sam@{utt} replaces @{tt}coder_sam@{utt}, @{tt}gamedev_okf@{utt} replaces @{tt}coder_okf@{utt}
sub-agents | via @{tt}gamedev_sam@{utt}: @{tt}coder_explorer@{utt}, @{tt}coder_reviewer@{utt}, @{tt}gamedev_tester@{utt}, @{tt}blender_agent@{utt}, @{tt}image_agent@{utt}
skills | always @{tt}gamedev-loop@{utt}; on demand @{tt}godot-conventions@{utt}, @{tt}asset-pipeline@{utt}, @{tt}adversarial-review@{utt}, @{tt}diagnosing-bugs@{utt}, @{tt}tdd@{utt}
knowledge | its own OKF bundle @{tt}data/okf/gamedev@{utt}, injected by the @{tt}gamedev_okf@{utt} hook; the coder's bundle hook is off
hooks off | @{tt}gamedev_sam.inject_sub_agent_context@{utt} (the agent asks for its sub-agents with a tool call instead of getting a list appended each time it changes)
prompt | @{tt}agents/prompts/gamedev.md@{utt}
@{body}

Its loop (the @{tt}gamedev-loop@{utt} skill): read the project, plan, edit
files, parse-check after every script edit, run headless, playtest in the
editor with a frozen clock, land assets, have the change reviewed. Its
prompt makes it report @{tt}Ran:@{utt}, @{tt}Played:@{utt},
@{tt}Check:@{utt} and @{tt}Reviewed:@{utt} lines. The Blender and image
agents work in sessions of their own, so their screenshots stay out of its
context; they hand back a file path, and landing that file in the project
is the @{tt}asset-pipeline@{utt} skill. See
@{" The Blender agent " link blender/agent} and
@{" The image agent " link image_compose/agent}.

@{h2}godot_agent

A @{tt}multi_turn_agent@{utt} without the harness, for "look at this scene
and tell me why the player falls through the floor". Tools:
@{tt}godot/*@{utt}, @{tt}media_ops/*@{utt}, @{tt}coder_fs/*@{utt} (to edit
@{tt}.tscn@{utt} and @{tt}.gd@{utt} files -- the addon cannot create
nodes), @{tt}skills/*@{utt}, @{tt}datetime/*@{utt}, and
@{tt}context_engineer/*@{utt} with its compaction hook on (scene trees and
logs are large). Skill on demand: @{tt}godot-conventions@{utt}. Models:
@{tt}or-claude-sonnet@{utt}, then @{tt}or-gemini-pro@{utt}, then
@{tt}or-gpt-full@{utt}; at most 80 steps.

@{tt}coder_fs@{utt} is the coder's instance: it reaches
@{tt}data/workspace/@{utt} and, as shipped, the whole checkout.

@{h2}gamedev_tester

@{tt}type: coder_tester@{utt} -- shell, read-only files, the tester's
cheap model chain -- plus the headless tools @{tt}godot_status@{utt},
@{tt}godot_check@{utt}, @{tt}godot_run@{utt} and @{tt}godot_script@{utt}.
No editor tools on purpose: it would be a second driver on the editor the
orchestrator plays in. Visibility @{tt}tool@{utt}: it is started as a
sub-agent, not picked in the chat. It runs checks and reports only the lines
that carry a failure.
@endnode

@node expect "What to expect"
@{h1}What to expect

@{bullet}@{b}"It ran" comes from the errors, not the exit code.@{ub} A
@{tt}push_error@{utt} at runtime leaves Godot's exit code at 0, so
@{tt}godot_run@{utt} reads the error blocks on stderr. A parse error under
@{tt}godot_check@{utt} does exit 1 and comes with @{tt}file:line@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}The addon cannot create nodes.@{ub} It reads, changes and
reparents what exists. New nodes come from editing the @{tt}.tscn@{utt} or
from @{tt}godot_script@{utt}; then @{tt}godot_scene reload@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Editor edits live in memory@{ub} until @{tt}godot_scene
save@{utt}; a file changed on disk is not seen until @{tt}reload@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}godot_play starts frozen.@{ub} Nothing moves until the agent
steps frames or milliseconds, which makes a playtest reproducible.
@{bullet}@{b}A freshly opened project sits on the 3D tab.@{ub} An editor
screenshot of a 2D game then shows an empty grid; pass @{tt}viewport:
"2d"@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}One client per editor.@{ub} A second agent on the same editor
gets @{tt}BUSY@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Screenshots are files.@{ub} The tools answer with a path;
the agent looks at it with @{tt}media_ops_load@{utt}
(@{" media_ops " link media_ops/load}).
@endnode

@node trouble "When something does not work"
@{h1}When something does not work

@{table}
The message | What it means
cannot start the Godot binary ... | @{tt}godot_binary@{utt} is wrong or not executable
no Godot editor is listening on 127.0.0.1:6550 | the project is not open in the editor, or has no addon yet: open it, or run setup first with the editor closed
BUSY: the addon refused ... another client is already connected | something else holds the editor (a second agent); close it or restart the editor
STALE: the addon closed the connection ... as idle | the command ran past the addon's 45 s activity window; the next call reconnects
TIMEOUT: <command> gave no answer within <n>s | the editor is busy or a step is too long; raise @{tt}long_timeout@{utt} or step in smaller pieces
PROTOCOL: <command>: the reply is not JSON | something other than the godot_mcp addon listens on that port
DISCONNECTED: the editor closed the connection ... | the editor or its game session ended during the command
NOT_RUNNING: No game is currently running ... | a game screenshot before @{tt}godot_play run@{utt}
CAPTURE_FAILED on a screenshot | the game could not capture its viewport, e.g. an editor running headless draws nothing
every headless tool reports empty output (Windows) | the plain @{tt}Godot.exe@{utt} instead of the @{tt}_console.exe@{utt}
@{body}
@endnode

@node status "status"
@{h1}status

Whether the binary answers @{tt}--version@{utt} (30 s limit) and whether an
editor with the addon answers a handshake (10 s). No parameters.

Answer: @{tt}binary@{utt} (@{tt}command@{utt}, @{tt}version@{utt},
@{tt}ok@{utt}, or @{tt}error@{utt}), @{tt}editor@{utt} (@{tt}host@{utt},
@{tt}port@{utt}, @{tt}reachable@{utt}; when reachable
@{tt}addon_version@{utt}, @{tt}godot_version@{utt},
@{tt}project_name@{utt}, @{tt}project_path@{utt}; otherwise
@{tt}hint@{utt} with the connection error), @{tt}projects_root@{utt},
@{tt}output_directory@{utt}. Always @{tt}status: success@{utt}; the status
line is an error line only when the binary is not usable. No editor is
information, not a failure.
@endnode

@node setup_tool "setup"
@{h1}setup

Prepares a project, editor closed. In this order: resolve the project below
the projects root (it may not exist yet); if there is no
@{tt}project.godot@{utt}, ask the binary for its version and write one
(@{tt}config_version=5@{utt}, the name, the main scene, the feature tag
from the version and "Forward Plus"); copy the addon into
@{tt}addons/godot_mcp@{utt} unless the same version is there; add it to
@{tt}[editor_plugins] enabled@{utt} (appended to an existing list, never
replacing it); run @{tt}--headless --import@{utt}; check that
@{tt}project.godot@{utt} now has the @{tt}MCPGameBridge@{utt} autoload.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
project | required; a name below the projects root, or a path inside it
name | project name for a @{i}new@{ui} project.godot; default the folder name
main_scene | main scene for a new project; @{tt}res://@{utt} is added to a project-relative path
force | copy the addon even when the same version is present
@{body}

@{tt}name@{utt} and @{tt}main_scene@{utt} may not hold a quote, a backslash
or a control character: they are written between quotes into
@{tt}project.godot@{utt}.

Answer: @{tt}project@{utt}, @{tt}created@{utt}, @{tt}addon@{utt}
("4.1.11 installed", "4.1.11 present", "4.1.11 reinstalled", "updated
<old> -> 4.1.11"), @{tt}plugin_enabled@{utt},
@{tt}autoload_registered@{utt}, @{tt}import@{utt} (@{tt}exit@{utt},
@{tt}timed_out@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt}, @{tt}stderr@{utt}).
@{tt}status@{utt} is @{tt}error@{utt} when the autoload did not land or the
import timed out. A binary that does not answer @{tt}--version@{utt} stops
setup before any @{tt}project.godot@{utt} is written.
@endnode

@node import_assets "import_assets"
@{h1}import_assets

Runs @{tt}--headless --import@{utt} on the project, so a file copied into
it (a @{tt}.glb@{utt}, @{tt}.png@{utt}, @{tt}.wav@{utt}) becomes usable.
Then, if an editor answers and has @{i}this@{ui} project open, it asks it to
@{tt}rescan_filesystem@{utt} so it sees the result. The rescan never
decides the verdict: it knows neither the project nor which file failed.

Parameter: @{tt}project@{utt} (required).

Answer: @{tt}ok@{utt} (exit 0, no timeout, no error blocks),
@{tt}exit@{utt}, @{tt}timed_out@{utt}, @{tt}editor_refreshed@{utt},
@{tt}editor_note@{utt} (why not: another project open, the rescan's
error; null when there is no editor), @{tt}errors@{utt},
@{tt}warnings@{utt}, @{tt}stderr@{utt}. The addon's own complaint that its
port is taken (an editor is open) is filed under warnings, not errors.
@endnode

@node check "check"
@{h1}check

Parse errors without running anything.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
project | required
script | one script (@{tt}res://...@{utt} or project-relative): @{tt}--check-only -s <script>@{utt}. Left out: every @{tt}.gd@{utt} in the project, skipping @{tt}addons/@{utt}, @{tt}.godot/@{utt} and hidden folders
@{body}

The whole-project check loads each script with a small SceneTree script
(@{tt}scripts/check_scripts.gd@{utt}) and counts those that cannot be
instantiated. A script that failed without printing a parse error (an
abstract class, a dependency that did not load) is still named, as an error
of kind @{tt}CHECK@{utt}.

Answer: @{tt}ok@{utt}, @{tt}scripts_checked@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt},
@{tt}warnings@{utt}, @{tt}exit@{utt}, @{tt}timed_out@{utt},
@{tt}output@{utt}, @{tt}stderr@{utt}. An error is @{tt}{kind, message,
file, line, at}@{utt}; one parse error counts once, though Godot prints two
blocks for it.
@endnode

@node run "run"
@{h1}run

Plays the project or one scene and reads what it printed.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
project | required
scene | scene to run (@{tt}res://@{utt} or project-relative); default the main scene
frames | quit after this many frames, default 60; 0 = until the game quits or the timeout
timeout | seconds before the process tree is killed; default @{tt}long_timeout@{utt}
windowed | open a real window instead of @{tt}--headless@{utt}
args | strings passed after @{tt}--@{utt} (the game reads them with @{tt}OS.get_cmdline_user_args()@{utt})
@{body}

The verdict: @{tt}timeout@{utt}, else @{tt}errors@{utt} if any error block
was printed, else @{tt}exit <n>@{utt} for a non-zero exit, else
@{tt}ok@{utt}. Answer: @{tt}verdict@{utt}, @{tt}exit@{utt},
@{tt}timed_out@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt}, @{tt}warnings@{utt},
@{tt}output@{utt} (stdout without the banner, at most 60,000 characters,
@{tt}truncated@{utt}), @{tt}stderr@{utt} (what no error block took: a
crash dump, @{tt}printerr()@{utt}). An error's location is the first
script frame of its backtrace, not the engine's C++ line.

@{code json}
{"project": "shmup", "scene": "levels/one.tscn", "frames": 120}
@{body}
@endnode

@node script "script"
@{h1}script

Runs GDScript against the project with the engine API and no editor: build
or rewrite scenes (@{tt}PackedScene.pack@{utt} +
@{tt}ResourceSaver.save@{utt}), batch changes, inspect resources.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
project | required
code | required; @{tt}extends SceneTree@{utt} is put in front when no line starts with @{tt}extends@{utt}. Put the work in @{tt}func _init():@{utt} and @{tt}print@{utt} what you want back
frames | iterations before Godot quits, default 1; raise it for code that awaits
timeout | seconds, default @{tt}long_timeout@{utt}
@{body}

The code goes into a temporary file that is deleted afterwards. It runs
unguarded: full GDScript, @{tt}OS.execute@{utt} included (see
@{" Paths, processes and the socket " link safety}).

Answer: @{tt}exit@{utt}, @{tt}timed_out@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt},
@{tt}warnings@{utt}, @{tt}exit_leaks@{utt} (Godot's "resources still in use
at exit" report -- normal for a one-shot script, kept apart so it neither
hides nor counts), @{tt}output@{utt}, @{tt}truncated@{utt},
@{tt}stderr@{utt}.
@endnode

@node export "export"
@{h1}export

Exports with a preset from the project's @{tt}export_presets.cfg@{utt}.
Needs the export templates for that Godot version.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
project | required
preset | required; the preset name exactly; may not start with @{tt}-@{utt}
filename | file below the output directory; default @{tt}<project>_<preset>.export@{utt}
debug | @{tt}--export-debug@{utt} instead of @{tt}--export-release@{utt}
@{body}

Success needs all three: the file changed, exit 0, no error block (Godot
writes the pack as it goes, so a killed or failed export leaves a file that
is bigger than before and still broken). Answer on success:
@{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}bytes@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt}, @{tt}warnings@{utt}.
Otherwise @{tt}status: error@{utt} with @{tt}error_type@{utt}
@{tt}MissingOutput@{utt} (nothing written) or @{tt}ExportFailed@{utt}
(written but not finished), plus @{tt}exit@{utt}, @{tt}errors@{utt},
@{tt}output@{utt}, @{tt}stderr@{utt}.
@endnode

@node scene "scene"
@{h1}scene

The scene open in the editor.

@{table}
Action | Addon command | Parameters
tree (default) | get_scene_tree | max_depth, max_children; 0 = no limit (default)
open | open_scene | scene_path, required
save | save_scene | scene_path only as "save as" for a scene without a file; another scene's path is refused (SCENE_MISMATCH)
reload | reload_scene | scene_path; default the current scene
@{body}

Paths get @{tt}res://@{utt} in front when they are project-relative.
Answer: @{tt}action@{utt} and the addon's @{tt}result@{utt} (at most
60,000 characters; a larger tree is cut and @{tt}truncated@{utt} says
where). Reload after any tool changed a file the editor has open, or the
editor saves its old copy back over it.
@endnode

@node node "node"
@{h1}node

Existing nodes of the open scene.

@{table}
Action | Addon command | Parameters
get | get_node_properties | node_path
find | find_nodes | name_pattern (glob) and/or type, root_path; searches the running game instead when one is playing
update | update_node | node_path, properties (a non-empty object)
reparent | reparent_node | node_path, new_parent_path
@{body}

Missing required values are refused before the editor is asked. Changes
are in memory until @{tt}godot_scene save@{utt}. Answer: @{tt}action@{utt},
@{tt}result@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"action": "update", "node_path": "Player", "properties": {"position": [100, 200], "visible": true}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node play "play"
@{h1}play

Runs the game from the editor and drives it.

@{table}
Action | Addon command | Parameters
run | run_project | scene_path, frozen (default true)
stop | stop_project |
freeze, thaw, status | game_time_freeze, _thaw, _status |
step | game_time_step | duration_ms or frames (one is required), inputs, report
step_until | game_time_step_until | until (required), max_ms, inputs, report
input | execute_input_sequence | inputs (required), report, screenshot_at_ms, screenshot_max_width
type_text | type_text | text (required), submit, delay_ms
exec | exec_run | source (required)
@{body}

@{bullet}@{b}step@{ub}: the addon caps @{tt}frames@{utt} at 1200 and
@{tt}duration_ms@{utt} at 300,000, but it also ends a step after 25 s of
real time and says so with @{tt}wall_budget_exceeded@{utt} -- step long
stretches in pieces.
@{bullet}@{b}step_until@{ub}: @{tt}until@{utt} is a GDScript expression
checked every frame; @{tt}max_ms@{utt} defaults to 20,000 (addon).
@{bullet}@{b}inputs@{ub}: objects on one timeline, e.g.
@{tt}{"action_name": "jump"}@{utt}, @{tt}{"action_name": "left",
"duration_ms": 800}@{utt}, @{tt}{"key": "ctrl+s"}@{utt},
@{tt}{"joy_button": "a"}@{utt}, @{tt}{"axis": "left_x", "value": -1.0,
"duration_ms": 500}@{utt}, @{tt}{"look": [dx, dy]}@{utt}; @{tt}start_ms@{utt}
places one on the timeline.
@{bullet}@{b}report@{ub}: expressions evaluated after the step, returned
with the result.
@{bullet}@{b}screenshot_at_ms@{ub}: captures inside an input sequence,
each written to @{tt}play_input_<n>.png@{utt} in the output directory; the
entry gets @{tt}path@{utt} instead of the image data.
@{tt}screenshot_max_width@{utt} is held to 128-4096 (addon default 640).
@{bullet}@{b}exec@{ub}: GDScript inside the running game. The addon scans
it for a list of calls that reach outside the game (@{tt}OS.execute@{utt},
@{tt}DirAccess@{utt}, file writes, ...) -- an accident guard, not a
boundary.

Answer: @{tt}action@{utt}, @{tt}result@{utt} (clipped as in scene).
@endnode

@node observe "observe"
@{h1}observe

Looks at the running game or the editor.

@{table}
Action | Parameters | What comes back
screenshot | source (game, default; editor), viewport (2d, 3d; editor only), filename, max_width | @{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}bytes@{utt}, @{tt}width@{utt}, @{tt}height@{utt}, @{tt}mesh_warnings@{utt}
logs | since, limit (50), severity (all, error, warning, info), clear | @{tt}messages@{utt}, @{tt}cursor@{utt}, @{tt}match_count@{utt}
state | paths, include, type, group (mcp_watch), max_nodes (40, at most 200) | live values of the selected nodes
stack | | the last error's stack trace
editor | | selection, open scene, play state
@{body}

@{bullet}@{b}screenshot@{ub}: written as a PNG below the output directory,
default @{tt}game.png@{utt} or @{tt}editor.png@{utt} (overwritten each
time); another extension becomes @{tt}.png@{utt}. @{tt}max_width@{utt} is
held to 128-4096, default 900. The data is checked to be a PNG
@{i}before@{ui} the file is touched, so a broken capture never destroys the
previous one.
@{bullet}@{b}logs@{ub}: pass the returned @{tt}cursor@{utt} as
@{tt}since@{utt} next time to get only what is new.

Defaults in brackets are the addon's. Answer (other than screenshot):
@{tt}action@{utt}, @{tt}result@{utt}.
@endnode

@node command "command"
@{h1}command

Any of the addon's 88 commands by name -- animation tracks and keyframes,
tilemap and gridmap cells, the profiler, selection, spatial info, resource
info, project settings, @{tt}rescan_filesystem@{utt},
@{tt}restart_editor@{utt} and the rest.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
command | required; e.g. @{tt}get_input_map@{utt}, @{tt}set_cell@{utt}, @{tt}get_spatial_info@{utt}
params | an object, passed as it is
long | use @{tt}long_timeout@{utt} instead of the command's usual timeout
@{body}

The reply is passed through, except that an image in it (a top-level
@{tt}image_base64@{utt}, or a @{tt}screenshots@{utt} list) is written to
@{tt}command_<name>_<n>.png@{utt} and replaced by its path. Nothing here
checks the parameters: @{tt}max_width@{utt} of a screenshot command, for
instance, is sent as given. Answer: @{tt}command@{utt}, @{tt}result@{utt}.
@endnode

@node safety "Paths, processes and the socket"
@{h1}Paths, processes and the socket

@{h2}Paths

A @{tt}project@{utt} must lie below @{tt}projects_root@{utt}; a file the
tools write (screenshot, export) below @{tt}output_directory@{utt}.
@{tt}..@{utt} that leads out, an absolute path elsewhere and a drive letter
are refused before Godot starts. Network and device paths
(@{tt}//host/share/...@{utt}, @{tt}//?/UNC/...@{utt}) are refused on their
text alone -- for projects, output files and the scene and script paths
handed to Godot -- because merely resolving such a path makes Windows sign
in to that host with your credentials, and Godot would load a scene from
it.

A scene or script path is taken as @{tt}res://@{utt}: a leading @{tt}/@{utt}
is the project's root. Only a path with a drive letter, or an absolute path
whose folder exists, goes to Godot as a file system path.

@{h2}Processes

Each headless tool starts one Godot process and waits for it. On a timeout
the plugin kills the process @{i}and everything it started@{ui} -- on
Windows the @{tt}_console.exe@{utt} is a wrapper around the real exe, and a
game may start processes of its own -- and answers with what was printed
so far. A stopped tool call kills its process the same way.

@{h2}Code that runs

@{tt}godot_script@{utt} runs any GDScript with the rights of this user:
files anywhere, @{tt}OS.execute@{utt}, the network. So does a game played
with @{tt}godot_run@{utt}. @{tt}play exec@{utt} has the addon's accident
guard and nothing more. Give @{tt}godot/*@{utt} only to an agent you would
let run code on this machine.

@{h2}The socket

Every editor call opens one WebSocket to @{tt}host:port@{utt}, sends one
@{tt}{"id", "command", "params"}@{utt}, reads one answer and closes. The
addon listens on @{tt}127.0.0.1@{utt} by default (its project settings
@{tt}godot_mcp/bind_mode@{utt} can change that) and asks for no password.
The addon accepts one client at a time and drops a connection 45 s after
the last message it received.

@{table}
Calls | Timeout
editor queries | @{tt}timeout@{utt} (60 s)
run_project, game_time_step, game_time_step_until, exec_run, execute_input_sequence, type_text, both screenshots, rescan_filesystem | @{tt}long_timeout@{utt} (600 s)
headless runs | @{tt}timeout@{utt} of run and script, else @{tt}long_timeout@{utt}; @{tt}--version@{utt} 30 s
connecting | 5 s
@{body}

An answer larger than 32 MB closes the connection (@{tt}DISCONNECTED@{utt}).
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Twelve tools from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, named after the
instance: @{tt}godot_status@{utt} ... @{tt}godot_command@{utt}. The
descriptions carry what a model gets wrong otherwise: that
@{tt}exit@{utt} is not the verdict, that @{tt}play@{utt} starts frozen,
that a screenshot is a path to load with media_ops, that a 2D game needs
@{tt}viewport: "2d"@{utt}, and to reload or save after edits.
@{tt}gamedev_tester@{utt} sees only four of them.

The agents' own tools (@{tt}gamedev_execute_task@{utt},
@{tt}godot_agent_execute_task@{utt}, @{tt}gamedev_tester_execute_task@{utt}
and their @{tt}list_available_tools@{utt}) are described by the
@{tt}self_tool_descriptions@{utt} in their YAML files.

@{h2}Errors

A failure answers @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "...", "error_type":
"..."}@{utt} and, for an addon error, @{tt}code@{utt} (the addon's own, or
@{tt}BUSY@{utt}, @{tt}STALE@{utt}, @{tt}TIMEOUT@{utt},
@{tt}DISCONNECTED@{utt}, @{tt}PROTOCOL@{utt}). Verbatim examples:

@{code}
'../x' resolves outside the projects root (<root>)
no project.godot in <path>; run the setup tool to create a project there
'//host/share/level.tscn' is on a network share; give a res:// path
'preset' must not start with '-' ('--path')
step needs 'duration_ms' or 'frames'
unknown action 'jump' (run, stop, freeze, thaw, status, step, step_until, input, type_text, exec)
@{body}

@{h2}The chat status line

One line per call, at most 140 characters (the middle is cut so the reason
at the end survives), for example:

@{code}
binary 4.7.2.stable.official, editor: 'Shmup' addon 4.1.11
shmup: 12 scripts parse clean
shmup levels/one.tscn: errors, 1 errors, 0 warnings, 3 lines out: res://main.gd:4: boom
shmup 'Windows' -> shmup.exe, 81234 KB
play step: stepped_ms=500, frames=30
game -> game.png, 900x506, 212 KB
@{body}

It is an error line when the call failed or its verdict is not clean.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Append-only: each call adds one tool result, nothing earlier is rewritten.
Results are bounded: stdout, trees and replies at 60,000 characters (lists
halved, long strings cut, @{tt}truncated@{utt} and @{tt}truncation@{utt}
say what went), pictures never inline -- they are files the model loads
when it wants to look.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Needs Godot 4.5 or later (the vendored addon 4.1.11 requires it) and
@{tt}websockets@{utt}, a core dependency. The instances ship in the
plugin's @{tt}agents/tools.yaml@{utt}, which @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}
includes with every plugin's @{tt}agents/*.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    godot:
      type: godot
      enabled: true
      description: "Godot 4: headless checks and runs of a project, and a running editor via the godot_mcp addon"
      godot_binary: "C:/prog/Godot/Godot_v4.7.2-stable_win64_console.exe"
      projects_root: "data/workspace"
      output_directory: "data/workspace/godot"
      timeout: 60
      long_timeout: 600
@{body}

The same file defines @{tt}gamedev_sam@{utt} (a sub-agent manager allowing
@{tt}coder_explorer@{utt}, @{tt}coder_reviewer@{utt},
@{tt}gamedev_tester@{utt}, @{tt}blender_agent@{utt},
@{tt}image_agent@{utt}; at most 4 per type and 20 per session, 3600 s
default wait) and @{tt}gamedev_okf@{utt} (the knowledge bundle in
@{tt}data/okf/gamedev@{utt}). An agent gets the tools with
@{tt}+godot/*@{utt}, or single tools with e.g.
@{tt}+godot/godot_check@{utt}.

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
godot_binary | godot | the executable; on Windows the console one
projects_root | data/workspace | every project must lie below it
output_directory | data/workspace/godot | screenshots and exports; created as needed
host | 127.0.0.1 | where the editor addon listens
port | 6550 | its port
timeout | 60 | seconds for editor queries
long_timeout | 600 | seconds for headless runs and long editor commands
@{body}

Relative directories count from the folder ScarabHive was started in, as
the file tools' do. The addon itself lives in the plugin's
@{tt}addon/godot_mcp@{utt} (upstream: satelliteoflove/godot-mcp, MIT;
version and commit in @{tt}addon/VENDORED.md@{utt}); @{tt}setup@{utt}
copies it into each project.
@endnode

@node gaps "Known gaps"
@{h1}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{b}The editor socket has no password.@{ub} Any program on this
machine that reaches the port can drive the editor and run GDScript in the
game with @{tt}exec_run@{utt} -- and so can a web page, if the browser lets
it open a WebSocket to localhost; the addon checks neither a token nor
the origin. Keep the editor closed, or the addon disabled, when you do not
use it.
@{bullet}@{b}The editor may have another project open.@{ub} The editor
tools act on whatever the editor shows; only @{tt}import_assets@{utt}
compares the project. @{tt}godot_status@{utt} names it.
@{bullet}@{b}A long step is cut at 25 s of real time.@{ub} The plugin does
not send the addon a longer wall budget; the result says
@{tt}wall_budget_exceeded@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}rescan_filesystem on a big project@{ub} can run past the
addon's 45 s idle window and end as @{tt}STALE@{utt}; the scan finishes
regardless.
@{bullet}@{b}An orphan is not killed.@{ub} The kill takes the process
tree as it stands; a process whose parent had already ended is no longer
part of it.
@{bullet}@{b}Screenshots overwrite each other@{ub} under their default
names; pass @{tt}filename@{utt} to keep one.
@endnode
